              question:
                'Explain software quality in the business context. How do cost of rework, customer satisfaction, reputation, competitiveness and business risk convert software quality into a business outcome?',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** A management-oriented question: why a business should pay for quality. The examiner wants five business consequences, each with a mechanism, not five adjectives.\n\n**Cost.** Rework, hotfixes and support are direct money; a defect found in production costs many times what it costs in review. Poor quality also lengthens the release cycle, which delays revenue.\n\n**Customer satisfaction and reputation.** Users judge the product by the failures they meet. Satisfaction drives renewals and referrals; reputation drives the price a company can charge, and a single public outage can erase years of goodwill.\n\n**Competitiveness.** Quality plus a short cycle is a market position: faster releases, fewer rollbacks, a lower price at the same margin.\n\n**Business risk.** Regulatory penalties, data loss, SLA credits and safety incidents are quality failures with legal consequences. Risk is the cost that is hardest to see until it happens.\n\n**The bridge.** Quality metrics (defect density, escaped defects, uptime, response time) translate into these five outcomes, which is how a test report becomes a business argument.\n\n**How to score the 6 marks.** Five outcomes with a mechanism each (4) · the metrics-to-business bridge (1) · a concluding judgement (1).',
              answer:
                '**Software quality in the business context** means judging quality not only as a technical property but as the factor that decides cost, revenue, reputation and risk for the organisation that ships the software.\n\n**1. Cost of rework and support**\nA defect costs more the later it is found: a few minutes in a review, hours in system testing, and days or weeks in production once support, hotfix, emergency release and customer compensation are counted. Poor quality also lengthens the release cycle, which delays the revenue that the release would have earned. Quality is therefore a direct line in the profit and loss account, not only a line in the test report.\n\n**2. Customer satisfaction**\nUsers do not measure coverage or cyclomatic complexity; they experience failures, slow pages and confusing screens. Satisfaction drives renewals, upsells and word-of-mouth acquisition, while a dissatisfied user is expensive to win back. Every escaped defect is a small withdrawal from that account.\n\n**3. Reputation and brand**\nA public outage or a data breach becomes a news item. Reputation affects the price a company can charge and the trust it is given in the next deal. Quality is one of the few ways a software business can defend a premium.\n\n**4. Competitive advantage**\nTwo products with the same features are separated by reliability, performance and release frequency. A team that can release weekly without rollbacks because its regression suite is trustworthy reaches the market earlier and spends less on firefighting. Quality is therefore a speed advantage, not only a cost.\n\n**5. Business and regulatory risk**\nIn banking, health, aviation and e-commerce, a defect can produce a regulatory penalty, a compensation claim, a service-credit under an SLA, or a safety incident. Risk exposure is the part of poor quality that the balance sheet does not show until it happens.\n\n**How quality metrics become a business argument**\n| Quality measure | Business translation |\n|---|---|\n| escaped defects per release | support cost, refunds, churn risk |\n| defect removal effectiveness | how much of the defect load reaches the customer |\n| response time and uptime | conversion rate and SLA compliance |\n| regression cycle time | how fast a fix can be shipped, and therefore how fast revenue arrives |\n| cost of poor quality | the money lost rather than invested |\n\n**Conclusion.** A test report that says 214 cases passed persuades nobody. A test report that says escaped defects fell from 24 to 9 and regression time fell from five days to one day is a business case, because it names the money and the risk that changed.\n\n**Exam line:** quality reduces rework cost, protects satisfaction and reputation, buys speed as a competitive advantage, and keeps regulatory and financial risk inside limits.',
