                'What is planning for software quality assurance? List the contents of an SQA plan and state who reviews and owns it.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** The SQA plan as a document: its purpose, its sections, and the ownership question. Keep it compact — four marks.\n\n**Purpose.** The plan states how quality will be assured on this project: goals, standards, reviews, testing, resources, schedule and records. It is written before execution and baselined under change control.\n\n**Contents.** Quality goals and metrics, applicable standards, organisation and responsibilities, documents and work products to be reviewed, review and test activities with entry and exit criteria, tools and environment, schedule, records and audit, risk and escalation.\n\n**Ownership.** Prepared by the SQA group or quality lead with the project manager, approved by senior management, audited against by the quality group. It is not the test plan — the test plan is one input to it.\n\n**How to score the 4 marks.** Purpose (1) · contents list (2) · ownership and review (1).',
              answer:
                '**Planning for software quality assurance** is the activity that produces the SQA plan — the document that states what quality means for this project, how it will be achieved, who is responsible, when each activity happens and how compliance will be shown.\n\n**Contents of an SQA plan**\n1. **Purpose and scope** — the project, the products covered and the quality objectives.\n2. **Quality goals and metrics** — measurable targets such as escaped defects per release, review coverage, test coverage and defect removal effectiveness.\n3. **Standards to be applied** — ISO 9001, CMMI process areas, internal coding and documentation standards, checklists.\n4. **Organisation and responsibilities** — the SQA group, reviewers, test lead, and the independence of the quality function.\n5. **Work products to be reviewed** — requirements, design, code, test cases, user documentation, release notes.\n6. **Review and test activities** — the type of review for each work product, the test levels, entry and exit criteria for each.\n7. **Tools, environment and records** — defect tracking, version control, CI, test management, and where quality records are stored.\n8. **Schedule** — review and test milestones aligned with the project plan.\n9. **Audit and compliance** — how and when the process will be audited.\n10. **Risk, escalation and change control** — what happens when a quality goal is missed, and how the plan itself is changed.\n\n**Who owns and reviews it**\n- **Prepared by** the SQA group or quality lead together with the project manager and the test lead.\n- **Reviewed by** the project manager, the technical lead and, where the contract requires it, the customer or an independent quality assurance function.\n- **Approved by** senior management, after which the plan is baselined.\n- **Audited against** by the SQA group during the project; deviations are recorded and escalated.\n\n**One distinction worth a mark:** the SQA plan is not the test plan. The test plan describes testing in detail; the SQA plan describes the whole quality system for the project, of which testing is one part.\n\n**Exam line:** the SQA plan sets quality goals, standards, responsibilities, reviews, tests, records and audits, and is owned by the quality group with management approval.',
