              question:
                'Explain the major quality assurance models. Describe ISO 9001, the CMMI maturity levels, Six Sigma, SPICE and the People CMM, and state how a project chooses between them.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** A survey question with structure: name the models, give each one purpose and a distinguishing feature, then say how selection works.\n\n**ISO 9001** — a requirements standard for a quality management system, audited by certification bodies; it says what a quality system must contain, not how to build software.\n\n**CMMI** — a process maturity model with five levels (initial, repeatable, defined, managed, optimising) and process areas; appraised, not certified. The level states how predictable the process is.\n\n**Six Sigma** — a defect-reduction methodology using DMAIC and a 3.4 defects per million target; data driven, with belts as roles.\n\n**SPICE (ISO 15504)** — process assessment with capability levels 0 to 5 per process, used for supplier evaluation.\n\n**People CMM** — maturity for the workforce: staffing, training, performance, team building.\n\n**Selection.** By the goal (certification, predictability, defect reduction, supplier assessment, people), by the customer contract, by cost and by the maturity of the organisation.\n\n**How to score the 6 marks.** Five models with purpose and one distinguishing feature each (4) · selection criteria (2).',
              answer:
                '**Quality assurance models** are structured frameworks that tell an organisation what a quality system should contain and how mature it is. They differ in purpose: some certify, some assess maturity, some drive defect reduction.\n\n**1. ISO 9001 — quality management system standard**\nA requirements standard for a quality management system, built on process approach, customer focus, leadership, continuous improvement and evidence-based decisions. An organisation is **certified** by an external body after an audit. It states what the system must contain, not how to write software, so it is often combined with a software-specific model. Its strength is market recognition, especially in contracts and export business.\n\n**2. CMMI — Capability Maturity Model Integration**\nA process maturity model with five levels:\n| Level | Name | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Initial | ad hoc, success depends on individuals |\n| 2 | Managed (repeatable) | basic project management, requirements and configuration control |\n| 3 | Defined | standard process tailored per project, organisation-wide |\n| 4 | Quantitatively managed | process measured and controlled statistically |\n| 5 | Optimising | continuous process improvement, defect prevention |\nIt is **appraised**, not certified, by trained assessors, and the appraisal covers process areas rather than the whole company. The level is a statement about predictability: at level 1 the outcome depends on who is on the team; at level 4 it can be predicted from data.\n\n**3. Six Sigma**\nA data driven methodology for reducing variation and defects, using DMAIC (define, measure, analyse, improve, control) for existing processes and DMADV for new ones. The target is 3.4 defects per million opportunities, which corresponds to a process shifted 1.5 sigma. Roles are belts — green, black, master black. It contributes to software quality through defect data analysis, root cause work and measurement discipline.\n\n**4. SPICE (ISO 15504) — process assessment**\nAssesses individual processes on capability levels 0 to 5 (incomplete to optimising). Used where a customer wants evidence about specific processes, such as requirement management or testing, rather than a single company level. It is popular for supplier evaluation and for automotive and medical software.\n\n**5. People CMM**\nA maturity model for the workforce rather than the process: staffing, communication, training, performance management, compensation, competency development, team building. It matters because most process failures in software are skill and staffing failures.\n\n**Other models worth one line:** Malcolm Baldrige (business excellence criteria), TMM and TMMi (testing maturity), Bootstrap and Trillium in Europe and Canada, and ISO/IEC 25010 for product quality characteristics.\n\n**How a project or company chooses**\n1. **By the goal** — market recognition and contract compliance point to ISO 9001; process predictability and large outsourcing to CMMI; defect reduction to Six Sigma; supplier assessment to SPICE; attrition and skill problems to People CMM.\n2. **By the customer** — many contracts name the model and even the level.\n3. **By cost and time** — certification and appraisal are expensive; a small product team may adopt the practices without the certificate.\n4. **By current maturity** — a level-1 organisation cannot jump to level 4; it fixes project management first.\n5. **Combination is normal** — ISO 9001 for the system, CMMI practices for the process, Six Sigma for specific defect problems.\n\n**Exam line:** ISO 9001 certifies the quality system, CMMI rates process maturity in five levels, Six Sigma attacks defects with data, SPICE rates individual processes, and People CMM rates the workforce.',
