              question:
                'A release recorded these quality costs. Prevention Rs 6 lakh, appraisal Rs 9 lakh, internal failure Rs 15 lakh, external failure Rs 20 lakh. Classify each cost, compute the cost of quality and the cost of poor quality, and explain what the mix says about the process.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** A numerical cost-of-quality question with an interpretation. Total the four categories, compute COPQ, then read the proportions.\n\n**Totals.** COQ = 6 + 9 + 15 + 20 = Rs 50 lakh. COPQ (failure only) = 15 + 20 = Rs 35 lakh. Prevention plus appraisal = Rs 15 lakh.\n\n**The mix.** 30 percent of the spend prevents and detects; 70 percent pays for defects that already happened, and 40 percent of the total is paid after release — the most expensive kind. That is an immature profile.\n\n**The reading.** External failure (Rs 20 lakh) exceeding prevention (Rs 6 lakh) means the process leaks. A rough rule: each rupee moved from failure to prevention returns several rupees.\n\n**Actions.** Reviews and requirement inspections, test design earlier, better regression coverage on the modules that escaped, and defect causal analysis of the external failures.\n\n**How to score the 6 marks.** Correct totals and percentages (3) · interpretation of the mix (2) · two concrete actions with the expected effect (1).',
              answer:
                '**1. Classification of the given costs**\n| Amount | Category | Why it belongs there |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rs 6 lakh | **Prevention** | training, process definition, review standards, static analysis — spent so defects are never created |\n| Rs 9 lakh | **Appraisal** | testing, inspections, test environment and test data — spent to detect defects that exist |\n| Rs 15 lakh | **Internal failure** | rework, re-testing, scrapped builds, idle time while a fix is made — defects found before release |\n| Rs 20 lakh | **External failure** | support, hotfix releases, refunds, SLA credits, lost goodwill — defects found by the customer |\n\n**2. Computation**\n- Cost of quality (COQ) = prevention + appraisal + internal failure + external failure = 6 + 9 + 15 + 20 = **Rs 50 lakh**\n- Cost of poor quality (COPQ) = internal failure + external failure = 15 + 20 = **Rs 35 lakh**\n- Conformance cost = prevention + appraisal = 6 + 9 = **Rs 15 lakh**\n\n**3. The mix in percentages**\n| Category | Amount | Share of COQ |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prevention | 6 | 12 percent |\n| Appraisal | 9 | 18 percent |\n| Internal failure | 15 | 30 percent |\n| External failure | 20 | 40 percent |\n\n**4. What the mix says about the process**\n- **70 percent of the quality spend is failure cost and only 30 percent is prevention plus appraisal.** The organisation is paying to clean up rather than to prevent — the classic immature profile.\n- **External failure is the largest single category at 40 percent.** Defects are being found by customers, which is the most expensive place to find them and also the most damaging to reputation.\n- **External failure (20) is more than three times prevention (6).** Whatever prevention exists is not covering the defect sources; the leak is at the front of the process — requirements and design — not at the test phase.\n- A mature profile inverts the shape: prevention and appraisal together are the majority, external failure is the smallest slice, and the **total** COQ is lower even though prevention spending is higher, because failure costs fall faster than prevention rises.\n\n**5. Two actions with the expected effect**\n1. **Requirement and design inspections plus mandatory code review** — moves money from internal failure to prevention. If prevention rises by Rs 4 lakh and internal failure falls by Rs 10 lakh, the net saving is immediate and the escaped defect count falls with it.\n2. **Defect causal analysis on the external failures and targeted regression automation on those modules** — attacks the 40 percent slice directly, and stops the same defect class from escaping again.\n\nA third lever worth a line: a release gate that blocks shipment while a severity-1 defect is open converts an external failure into an internal one, which is cheaper by an order of magnitude.\n\n**Exam line:** COQ = Rs 50 lakh, COPQ = Rs 35 lakh, and with 70 percent of the money going into failure the process is prevention-poor.',
