                'A release removed defects as follows: requirements review 20, design review 30, code review 25, unit testing 35, system testing 40, and 20 defects were reported in production. Compute DRE for the release and for each phase, and state which phase needs attention.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** A phase-wise DRE calculation with a diagnosis. Total the defects, compute the release DRE, then the share each phase removed.\n\n**Totals.** Removed before release = 20 + 30 + 25 + 35 + 40 = 150. Escaped = 20. Total = 170. Release DRE = 150 / 170 x 100 = 88.2 percent — the fair band.\n\n**Phase shares.** Requirements review 11.8 percent, design review 17.6 percent, code review 14.7 percent, unit 20.6 percent, system 23.5 percent, escaped 11.8 percent.\n\n**Diagnosis.** The two reviews before coding removed 50 of 170, about 29 percent — that is late-arriving defect detection, since defects found in review are cheapest. System testing carrying the largest share (23.5 percent) means defects live until the last phase. The escaped 11.8 percent is close to what the requirements review caught, which is a poor ratio.\n\n**Improvement.** Stronger requirement and design inspection with checklists, review rate control and defect causal analysis on escaped defects; the target is to move the peak of the curve to the left.\n\n**How to score the 6 marks.** Correct totals and release DRE (2) · phase-wise table with percentages (2) · diagnosis of the weak phase (1) · improvement action (1).',
              answer:
                '**1. Totals**\n- Removed before release = 20 + 30 + 25 + 35 + 40 = **150**\n- Escaped to production = **20**\n- Total defects = 150 + 20 = **170**\n\n**2. Defect removal effectiveness for the release**\nDRE = (defects removed before release / total defects) x 100 = (150 / 170) x 100 = **88.2 percent**\n\nThat falls in the **fair** band: above 90 percent good, 80 to 89 percent fair, 70 to 79 percent poor, below 70 percent most defects escaping.\n\n**3. Phase-wise removal**\n| Phase | Defects removed | Share of all 170 defects |\n|---|---|---|\n| Requirements review | 20 | 11.8 percent |\n| Design review | 30 | 17.6 percent |\n| Code review | 25 | 14.7 percent |\n| Unit testing | 35 | 20.6 percent |\n| System testing | 40 | 23.5 percent |\n| Escaped to production | 20 | 11.8 percent |\n\n**4. Which phase needs attention**\n- **This is a late-detection profile.** The three review activities removed 75 defects, 44 percent, while the two test phases removed 75 as well — but a defect removed in a review costs a fraction of the same defect removed in system testing, so the same count at a later phase is far more expensive.\n- **The pre-coding reviews are the weakest link.** Requirements and design review together removed 50 defects, only 29 percent of the total, and the requirements review removes less than the number that escapes to production. Requirement defects are the most expensive to fix once they reach testing, because they invalidate designs and test cases.\n- **System testing carries the largest share (23.5 percent).** That is a sign that the earlier gates are letting defects through instead of stopping them; the last phase should not be the busiest.\n- Escaped defects at 11.8 percent mean one defect in eight reaches the customer — around 20 support incidents from one release.\n\n**5. Improvement actions**\n1. **Formal inspection with checklists at the requirement and design phases**, with a controlled review rate (about 200 lines or one document section per hour) and recorded defect data per review.\n2. **Defect causal analysis on the 20 escaped defects** to find whether they came from missing requirements, missed design cases or thin regression coverage, and feed the result back into the review checklists.\n3. **Earlier test design** — test cases written from the requirement review, so a missing rule is found before coding starts.\n4. **Shift the target curve left**: the goal is not more defects removed at system testing, but the same total removed earlier, with an escaped figure below 5 percent.\n\n**Exam line:** release DRE is 88.2 percent, the profile is late-heavy, and the requirement and design reviews — not the test phases — are where the next improvement belongs.',
