                'State the seven principles of software testing and explain any two of them with an example.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** The principles from Unit II, then two explained with an example each. Four marks — the list carries most of the credit.\n\n**The seven.** Testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence; exhaustive testing is impossible; early testing saves time and money; defect clustering (Pareto); the pesticide paradox (tests stop finding defects); testing is context dependent; the absence-of-errors fallacy (a bug-free product that does not meet the need is still a failure).\n\n**Two explained.** Pesticide paradox — the same regression suite stops finding new defects, so cases must be reviewed and extended. Defect clustering — a few modules carry most defects, so risk-based allocation of effort is rational.\n\n**How to score the 4 marks.** Seven principles named correctly (2.5) · two explained with an example (1.5).',
              answer:
                '**The seven principles of software testing**\n1. **Testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence.** Testing reduces the probability of undiscovered defects but cannot prove that none remain.\n2. **Exhaustive testing is impossible.** Every combination of inputs cannot be executed, except in trivial cases; risk analysis and prioritisation replace exhaustion.\n3. **Early testing saves time and money.** Testing activities should start as early as possible, at the requirement stage, because a defect found in a document costs a fraction of the same defect found in production.\n4. **Defect clustering.** A small number of modules contain most of the defects, following the Pareto principle, so effort is best concentrated where the defect history is heaviest.\n5. **The pesticide paradox.** If the same tests are repeated unchanged, they eventually stop finding new defects; the suite has to be reviewed and new cases added.\n6. **Testing is context dependent.** A banking application, a game and a medical device are tested in different ways; there is no single correct approach.\n7. **Absence-of-errors fallacy.** Finding and fixing every defect does not help if the system does not meet the user needs — a bug-free product built to the wrong requirement is a failed product.\n\n**Two principles explained**\n\n**Defect clustering, with an example.** In a release with 120 defects, 78 of them sit in three of the twenty modules — the payment module, the slot booking module and the notification service. Those three modules are large, changed often and integrated with external systems. The practical consequence is that test effort, code review and automation are concentrated there, while the remaining modules receive a smoke test only. Clustering also means that code review of a module with a heavy defect history returns far more per hour than review spread evenly across the codebase.\n\n**The pesticide paradox, with an example.** A login regression suite written two years ago still passes on every build and has not found a defect in eight months. It is not proving that login is safe; it is proving that the suite only tests what was thought of two years ago — nothing about password reset by email link, single sign-on, rate limiting or account lockout timers. The fix is to review the suite every quarter against change history and escaped defects, retire cases that can no longer fail, and add cases for the new risks.\n\n**The other principles in one line each.** Exhaustive testing is impossible, so a 30-field form with three values per field gives 2 followed by fourteen zeros combinations and risk decides what is tested. Early testing is cheapest because a requirement defect fixed during review costs minutes and the same defect fixed in production costs days. Context matters because a medical device needs audit trails and traceability that a marketing site does not. And the absence-of-errors fallacy explains why acceptance testing exists at all, even after a clean system test.\n\n**Exam line:** presence not absence, exhaustive impossible, early testing, defect clustering, pesticide paradox, context dependence and the absence-of-errors fallacy.',
