              question:
                'An insurance premium calculator takes age (18 to 65), sum insured (Rs 1,00,000 to Rs 50,00,000) and policy term (1 to 30 years), and applies a 10 percent discount when age is below 30 and the term is at least 20 years. Design the domain tests: list the sub-domains, the boundary values and the interaction cases.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** An applied domain testing question: three variables with ranges plus one interaction rule. Build the table, then the test set, then explain what each case targets.\n\n**Sub-domains.** For each variable — below minimum, valid, above maximum, and invalid type or format. Age: below 18, 18 to 65, above 65. Sum insured: below 1 lakh, 1 lakh to 50 lakh, above 50 lakh. Term: below 1, 1 to 30, above 30.\n\n**Boundary values.** Age 17, 18, 19, 64, 65, 66. Sum 99999, 100000, 100001, 4999999, 5000000, 5000001. Term 0, 1, 2, 29, 30, 31.\n\n**Interaction cases.** The discount rule needs all four combinations of (age below 30 true or false) and (term at least 20 true or false) — four cases. Plus the pair at the exact edges: age 29 and term 20 gives the discount, age 30 and term 20 does not.\n\n**Discipline.** One invalid value per case, all other fields valid, so the failure is unambiguous.\n\n**How to score the 6 marks.** Sub-domain table for three variables (2) · boundary values (2) · four interaction cases plus the two edge pairs (1.5) · one-invalid-value rule (0.5).',
              answer:
                '**1. Sub-domains for each variable**\n| Variable | Below minimum | Valid | Above maximum | Invalid input |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Age | below 18 | 18 to 65 | above 65 | blank, decimal, text, negative |\n| Sum insured | below 1,00,000 | 1,00,000 to 50,00,000 | above 50,00,000 | blank, text, comma format, negative |\n| Policy term | below 1 | 1 to 30 | above 30 | blank, decimal, text |\n\n**2. Boundary values to use**\n| Variable | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Age | 17, 18, 19, 29, 30, 64, 65, 66 |\n| Sum insured | 99,999 · 1,00,000 · 1,00,001 · 49,99,999 · 50,00,000 · 50,00,001 |\n| Policy term | 0, 1, 2, 19, 20, 29, 30, 31 |\n\n**3. Nominal values inside each sub-domain**\nAge 40 · sum insured 10,00,000 · term 10 — the baseline case that must always pass. Every invalid case is built by breaking exactly one field and keeping the other two at these nominal values.\n\n**4. Interaction cases for the discount rule**\nThe rule is: discount applies when (age below 30) AND (term at least 20). All four truth combinations are needed.\n| Case | Age | Term | Age below 30 | Term at least 20 | Expected discount |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | 25 | 25 | true | true | 10 percent |\n| I2 | 25 | 10 | true | false | none |\n| I3 | 40 | 25 | false | true | none |\n| I4 | 40 | 10 | false | false | none |\n\n**5. Edge pairs of the interaction**\n- Age 29 with term 20 — both conditions at their last and first valid point, discount must apply.\n- Age 30 with term 20 — one day or one year past the limit, discount must not apply.\n- Age 29 with term 19 — the other side of the same pair, discount must not apply.\n\nThese three cases catch the two classic defects: a strict comparison used where a non-strict one is required, and the wrong variable in the second half of the condition.\n\n**6. Additional cases worth writing**\n- **Type and format** — age 30.5, sum insured entered as 10,00,000 with commas, term as 2 years 6 months.\n- **Cross-field sanity** — a term longer than the age allows (age 18 with a 30 year term), and a sum insured that breaches the underwriting limit for the age band.\n- **Currency and rounding** — premium calculated at 10 percent discount on an odd amount, checked for rounding direction.\n\n**7. How the set is executed**\nOne invalid value per test case, all other fields nominal, so that a failure names its own cause. Expected premium is computed by hand from the rate table before execution, never read from the application.\n\n**Exam line:** three sub-domain tables, six boundary values per variable, four interaction cases plus the three edge pairs, and one invalid field per case.',
              diagram: 'st3-domain',
